  "text": "Numbers held a mysterious and mighty seat in Celtic thought. Three and five ranked as the most vital of the count. The tally skill reached ten, the sum of two hands, and above that ran as a crowd. Old forms of Irish hint at a count in fives. The number two stood for duty and devotion. Four meant wholeness, a shadow of a missing fifth. Seven carried the sign of magic. A man born to a seventh son, in a line of seven, was seen as gifted. Transformation tales run seven years, when Étain lived as an insect and Lough Gur falls dry to show its secret.",
  "okr": {
    "objective": "Relate the mystical weights the Celts put on their numbers.",
    "key_results": [
      "The numbers three and five were the most important to the Celts.",
      "Their tally reached ten, the sum of two hands, and above that ran as a crowd.",
      "Seven tied to magic and the seventh son of a seventh son was thought born enchanted.",
      "Change tales run seven years, when Étain lived as an insect and Lough Gur falls dry."
    ],
    "evidence": "Monaghan, Encyclopedia of Celtic Mythology and Folklore"
